 Routes\n 226 \n...\n 235 ## Firebase & Security\n 236 \n 237 - **Firestore rules** are in `firestore.rules` — always deny by default, whitelist explicitly\n...\n 246 ---\n 247 \n 248 ## RAG System\n 249 \n 250 - Vector store: Qdrant (default collection: `arkan_vectors`, dimension: 768)\n...\n 258 ---\n 259 \n 260 ## Internationalization (i18n)\n 261 \n 262 - Translation keys are in `src/locales/en.json` and `src/locales/ar.json`\n...\n 267 ---\n 268 \n 269 ## Testing\n 270 \n 271 - Test files live alongside source code in `__tests__/` subdirectories\n...\n 279 ---\n 280 \n 281 ## Important Files\n 282 \n 283 | File | Purpose |\n...\n 298 ---\n 299 \n 300 ## Environment Variables (key ones)\n 301 \n 302 ```\n...\n 328 ---\n 329 \n 330 ## Common Pitfalls\n 331 \n 332 - Do not import `firebase-admin` or any server-only module in client components or hooks\n 333 - Do not use `new` to instantiate services — always resolve via `container.resolve()`\n 334 - All Genkit flow files must declare `\'use server\'` at the top\n 335 - **Client-Server SDK Decoupling**: AI model names and task constants (`AiModels.ts`) must be located in a dependency-free domain folder. Importing them from server-only SDK entry points (like `Genkit.ts`) pulls heavy Node.js dependencies into the browser bundle, causing Turbopack errors.\n 336 - **Node.js Built-in Stubs**: When using libraries that transitively depend on Node.js built-ins (e.g., `perf_hooks`, `net`), ensure they are stubbed in `next.config.ts` using `turbopack.resolveAlias` for the browser bundle.\n 337 - **Turbopack `resolveAlias` paths**: When setting `turbopack.resolveAlias` for browser stubs in `next.config.ts`, use the `{ browser: \'./path/to/stub\' }` syntax with a **relative path** (starting with `./`). This ensures the alias applies only to the client bundle and resolves correctly in Turbopack. Using absolute paths or global strings can cause server-side crashes (e.g., `path.join is not a function`).\n 338 - Server-only DI registrations must be inside the `typeof window === \'undefined\'` guard in `container.ts`\n 339 - **Isolated Modules and Decorators**: In classes using `tsyringe` decorators (like `@injectable()`), always import domain interfaces using `import type` (e.g., `import { type IUserRepository } ...`). This prevents "type referenced in a decorated signature" errors when `isolatedModules` and `emitDecoratorMetadata` are enabled.\n 340 - **Infrastructure Purity**: Ensure Domain Repository Interfaces (e.g., `IIndexedDocumentRepository.ts`) do not import infrastructure-specific types (like Firebase `Timestamp`). Use native JS types (`Date`, `string`) and handle conversions in the infrastructure implementation.\n 341 - **Hook Order Safety**: Never return early from a React component if there are hooks (useState, useEffect, etc.) defined below that return. This causes "Rendered fewer hooks than expected" errors. Always call all hooks at the top level before any conditional logic.\n 342 - **Keyboard Shortcut Pattern**: For IDE-style interfaces, centralize shortcut logic in a module-specific hook (e.g., `src/modules/*/adapters/hooks/useKeyboardShortcuts.ts`). This hook should handle platform-specific modifiers (Cmd vs Ctrl) and prevent default browser behavior for common shortcuts like `Cmd+S`.\n 343 - **Subagent Delegation**: Even for localized UI enhancements or hook implementations, strictly follow the delegation policy. Use the **Next.js 15 AI Development Assistant** for hook logic and **shadcn/ui Component Builder Assistant** for extending toolbar components. This ensures consistency with the latest framework patterns.\n 344 - **Fullscreen Transitions**: When implementing fullscreen modes for document previews, use a `ref` on the root container and manage the `isFullscreen` state via `fullscreenchange` event listeners. Apply `fixed inset-0 z-[100]` classes conditionally to ensure the preview occupies the entire viewport above all other UI layers.\n 345 - **Centralized State Management for Multi-Zone Layouts**: For complex IDE-style pages, centralize visibility and interaction states (e.g., `isOutlineVisible`, `activeChapterId`, `zoomLevel`) in a single domain hook or store. This ensures synchronization across disparate UI zones like floating HUDs, sidebars, and the primary canvas, and allows global keyboard shortcuts to trigger UI changes consistently.\n 346 - **Large Refactor Strategy**: For massive component rewrites or structural UI changes, prefer using `create_file` with `overwrite: true` instead of stacking multiple `find_and_replace_code` calls. This drastically reduces "No matches found" errors and syntax mismatches in complex JSX blocks.\n 347 - Keep domain entities, shared types, and any server-reachable modules free of client-only dependencies (for example drag-and-drop or React UI libraries); they can crash API routes at runtime when imported transitively\n 348 - When adding new TanStack Query data fetches, always add a key factory to `src/lib/query-keys.ts`\n 349 - Credits are deducted server-side only — never trust client-side credit values\n 350 - RTL/LTR layout changes must be tested in both Arabic and English\n 351 - **String Spreading Bug**: In use cases or controllers, never use the spread operator (`...`) on a variable that might be a string (e.g., `return { ...result, metadata }`). Spreading a string results in an object with numeric keys (`{ "0": "h", "1": "e" ... }`). Always check `typeof result === \'string\'` before attempting to merge with metadata.\n 352 - **Discriminator Field Collisions**: Do not reuse a business/domain field like `type` as a Zod discriminated-union key if the entity already uses that field for another meaning (for example section content category vs. content-item kind). Use a dedicated discriminator such as `itemType` or `kind` to avoid save/validation failures.\n 353 - **Schema/Entity Contract Sweep**: When changing domain serialization (`toProperties`, `withProps`, readonly fields, discriminator keys), sweep API validators, repository mappings, seed data, and client payload producers in the same pass. Partial updates tend to surface later as save-route validation errors rather than obvious type errors.\n 354 - **Plan-Mode Tool Discipline**: Before calling plan-only tools like `exit_plan_mode`, verify the latest system reminder still indicates PLAN mode. After an approval/transition or any long multi-step exchange, re-check the current mode instead of assuming it is unchanged.\n 355 \n 356 ## Notes\n 357 \n 358 - When converting function-based modules (especially `adapters/api-client/*`) to class-based DI services, treat it as a 3-part refactor: update the module, register it in `src/infrastructure/di/container.ts`, then update every consumer/import in hooks, stores, and components. Skipping the consumer sweep leaves the codebase in a mixed pattern.\n 359 - When relocating UI controls between panels (e.g., moving section-specific tabs or actions to a right sidebar), lift the active selection state to the highest shared owner (such as `useReportGenerator`) and pass it down through the panel component. Remove the original control from list items to avoid duplicated actions and inconsistent state.\n 360 - Before doing bulk `find_and_replace_code` changes, inspect the exact file contents with `open_files`/`expand_code_chunks` first. Several files in this project look similar but differ slightly in imports or surrounding code, so guessing exact match strings causes avoidable failed edits.\n 361 - **shadcn/ui Wrapper Re-exports**: The local `src/components/ui/resizable.tsx` (and similar shadcn wrappers) do not always re-export every type from the underlying library (e.g., `ImperativePanelHandle` from `react-resizable-panels`). Before using a type from a wrapped component, grep the local wrapper file to verify it is exported. If not, import it directly from the upstream package or fall back to `any` for `forwardRef` generics.\n 362 - Before refactoring a client/helper, `grep` for real call sites first. Some adapters or imports may be unused, partially migrated, or temporarily stubbed in the UI, so updating presumed consumers without verifying usage wastes time and creates avoidable churn.\n 363 - If a large route/module needs a structural rewrite and targeted replacements keep failing, prefer reading the full file and then replacing the whole file in one controlled edit instead of stacking many brittle partial replacements.\n 364 - Keep heavy export/conversion logic out of hooks and route handlers. Prefer thin hooks/routes that delegate to infrastructure services; wrap existing converter libraries/utilities behind injectable services instead of duplicating conversion logic in UI code.\n 365 - Do not use `any` as the first fix for `tsyringe` / `TypeInfo not known for "undefined"` errors. Fix the root cause instead: centralize metadata setup, use `@inject(delay(() => ...))` for controller constructor params, and verify import paths / initialization order.\n 366 - When sweeping for direct Firebase usage, distinguish active code from commented-out legacy snippets before planning refactors. Commented imports/usages are not architectural violations and should not drive new work.\n 367 - If a service module grows into a data-access concern (for example document tracking in RAG), promote it into an explicit repository interface + infrastructure implementation rather than leaving it as a free-floating helper.\n 368 - When converting frontend API clients or service adapters to return `Result` objects, do the consumer sweep in the same pass: update TanStack Query hooks, mutations, and route callers to unwrap success values and `throw result.error` on failure so existing `onError`/error-boundary flows keep working.\n 369 - Preserve existing public API response shapes during Result-pattern migrations unless you are also updating every caller. Some endpoints (especially auth/session/signup flows) may intentionally return nested payloads that the frontend already depends on, so swapping them to a generic `handle*Result(...)` response can introduce subtle breakage.\n 370 - When adding Jest coverage in this repo, prefer mocking repositories to return `Result.ok(...)` / `Result.fail(...)` directly rather than constructing heavy domain objects unless the entity behavior is what you are testing. Also verify Jest path aliases cover `@/modules/*`, and keep `jest.setup.js` CommonJS-compatible in the current setup.\n 371 - For large architecture migrations, prefer staged compatibility shims before deleting old barrels. A temporary re-export layer (for example in `src/lib/types.ts`) can keep the app compiling while imports are migrated module-by-module.\n 372 - Avoid repo-wide path rewrites or broad shell replacements until you have verified the exact import patterns with targeted searches. Large `sed`/mass-replace passes can silently create widespread breakage, especially across `src/modules/**/adapters/api-client`, scripts, tests, and case-sensitive file names.\n 373 - After moving shared infrastructure such as API clients, Firebase initialization, or RAG utilities, immediately sweep for old import roots and run `npm run typecheck` and `npm run lint` early. These migrations tend to fail from stale imports more than from business-logic bugs.\n 374 - When moving files between folders with naming changes, standardize internal imports in the same pass (for example `./Template` vs `./template`, or moved exporter/helper paths). Case mismatches are easy to miss and show up late as type or lint failures.\n 375 - Keep domain entities free of framework/browser concerns and avoid circular class dependencies between entities. When two entities need to reference each other, prefer a lightweight `*Properties` interface or ID-based reference rather than importing the full class both ways.\n 376 - After overwriting critical files or doing bulk moves, reopen the changed files immediately to verify the actual content on