 and keeps response-shape preservation easier.\n 383 - When standardizing API-client or internal repository contracts (e.g., migrating to the Result Object Pattern), treat it as a full slice migration across all layers:\n 384     1.  **Domain**: Define module-specific Errors (e.g., `AdminError`) and update Repository Interfaces.\n 385     2.  **Infrastructure**: Update Repository implementations and add a `handle{Module}Result` helper to `api-utils.ts`.\n 386     3.  **Application**: Refactor Use Cases and Controllers to propagate the Result objects.\n 387     4.  **API/Frontend**: Update route handlers and logic hooks to consume the new Result shape.\n 388 - Partial migrations leave mixed raw-return/Result-return patterns that are easy to misuse and break type safety.\n 389 - **Database Migration Insights (NoSQL to SQL)**:\n 390     - When migrating from NoSQL (Firestore) to SQLite (Drizzle), use `text` columns for JSON fields (arrays, nested objects) and handle serialization/deserialization explicitly in the repository layer to maintain domain entity compatibility.\n 391     - After changing the database provider, immediately update the DI container (`src/infrastructure/di/container.ts`) and run `npm run typecheck` to catch method signature or argument order mismatches.\n 392     - **Repository Interface Verification**: Before implementing new repositories during a technology switch, always cross-reference the exact method signatures in the Domain Repository Interfaces (`src/modules/*/domain/repositories/*.ts`). SQL and NoSQL repository implementations often differ in argument order or data mapping, and catching these early saves time during the typecheck/fix cycle.\n 393     - **API Client Synchronization**: When updating domain interfaces (e.g., adding dashboard metrics), remember to update the corresponding interface in the `api-client` adapter. Unlike a direct repository change, the frontend often relies on these client-side interface definitions, and missing them causes `tsc` failures in components.\n 394 - **Injectable Utilities**: Cross-cutting utilities (Cache, Cookie, Session management) should be promoted to injectable services with domain-level interfaces. This allows for clean dependency injection, better test mocking, and consistent architectural patterns across the application.\n 395 - **Domain Entity ID Generation**: Domain entities (e.g., `Project`, `Chapter`, `Section`) should not implicitly generate their own IDs (e.g., via `uuidv4` in the constructor). This ensures hydration is predictable. IDs should be generated by the repository for new entities or passed explicitly from the application layer.\n 396 - **Intelligence Suite Aesthetics (IDE Pattern)**:\n 397     - **Typography Matrix**: Use high-density technical labels (`text-[10px]` or `text-[11px]`, `font-black`, `uppercase`, `tracking-[0.15em]` to `[0.3em]`) for UI controls and metadata. Contrast this with a premium serif stack (`text-[16px]`, `leading-[1.75]`) for AI-generated prose.\n 398     - **Glassmorphism & Depth**: Utilize `bg-background/40`, `backdrop-blur-2xl`, and `border-primary/10` for panels. For deep immersion, use multi-layered shadows (e.g., `shadow-[0_0_80px_-20px_...]`) on primary document canvases.\n 399     - **3-Zone Workspace Architecture**: When building complex editing interfaces, follow the "IDE Pattern": Top Zone (Session Actions), Left Zone (Files/Setup), Center Zone (Document Canvas), Right Zone (AI Tools/Properties).\n 400     - **Contextual HUD Pattern**: For document-specific utility controls (like Zoom or Page Navigation), use a semi-transparent floating glassmorphism HUD (`backdrop-blur-xl`, `bg-background/60`) at the bottom-center. This keeps primary toolbars clean.\n 401     - **Collapsible Sidebar Hierarchy**: Manage large project trees by implementing animated collapsible chapters (using `Framer Motion`\'s `AnimatePresence`). This allows users to focus on specific sections without sidebar clutter.\n 402 - When tightening TanStack Query key design, avoid placeholder scopes like shared detail keys if the data is actually user-scoped. Update invalidation/removal call sites in the same pass so cache behavior stays coherent.\n 403 - For Jest in this repo, React hook/context tests may need environment support beyond the default config: ensure the `@/` alias is mapped, JSX in `.tsx` tests is transformed correctly, `.next/` is ignored to avoid haste collisions, and `reflect-metadata` is loaded before code that imports `tsyringe`.\n 404 - When a hook/unit test pulls in a heavy domain entity with ESM-only or parser-heavy transitive imports, prefer mocking that entity/module at the test boundary rather than widening Jest transforms to chase unrelated runtime dependencies.\n 405 - For small authenticated client-side mutations that do not yet justify a dedicated API client abstraction, prefer `apiService({ firebaseUser, ... })` over hand-written `getIdToken()` + `fetch(...)` logic. It keeps auth/header/error handling consistent and makes later migrations easier.\n 406 - In Jest, make mocks match the exact import path used by the module under test. If the hook imports `../../core/useBuilderStore`, mocking a different alias or relative path will not intercept the dependency and can lead to confusing failures.\n 407 - When `find_and_replace_code` reports multiple matches inside the same file, stop retrying the same short snippet. Reopen or expand the file and anchor the replacement with adjacent unique context such as the surrounding method signature or neighboring lines.\n 408 - **Pruned Tool Results / Large File Reading**: When opening multiple large files simultaneously with `open_files` or `expand_code_chunks`, tool results may be pruned from message history before you can act on them. To avoid losing file content mid-task: open only one large file at a time when you need to immediately reference its content, or batch all reading into a single iteration before any editing begins. If results were pruned, re-open the specific file rather than proceeding from memory.\n 409 - **Parallel `create_file` Calls Can Fail**: When invoking two `create_file` calls in the same tool invocation block, the second call can fail with a JSON parse error (e.g., `EOF while parsing an object`). To avoid this, write files sequentially — complete one `create_file` call per iteration when creating multiple files. Batching reads is safe; batching writes is not.\n 410 - **`grep` as a File Finder**: When you only need the file path (not the content), use `grep` with `content_pattern="."` and a `path_glob`. If the file has more than ~100 lines, grep will respond with "Too many matches — showing matched files only", which is exactly what you need. This is faster than `expand_folder` for targeted path discovery across deep directory trees.\n 411 - **Feature Toggle Pattern in Toolbars**: When adding a boolean visibility toggle to a shared toolbar (e.g., `showCoverPage`), add `show{Feature}: boolean` and `set{Feature}: (v: boolean) => void` to the props interface, pass state down from the parent, and use `bg-primary/10 text-primary` for the active state to match existing toolbar button conventions in this codebase.\n 412 - **AnimatePresence for Optional Document Sections**: When a document section (like a cover page) can be toggled on/off above the main canvas, wrap it in `<AnimatePresence>` + `<motion.div>` with `opacity/y/scale` variants inside a single `flex flex-col items-center` wrapper. This keeps the document body stable while the optional section slides in/out smoothly.\n 413 - **Feature + Settings Panel Co-toggle Pattern**: When a toolbar button enables a feature (e.g., cover page) that has an associated floating configuration panel, use a single `handleToggle{Feature}(visible)` handler that sets both `show{Feature}` and `show{Feature}Settings` together. The panel\'s own `×` close button should only dismiss the settings panel (not the feature itself), so users can keep the