rn like `position` vs `side`, missing required props, or stale wrapper assumptions after the structural refactor is already done.\n 420 - During typing cleanups, start with low-risk replacements that reuse existing domain contracts: controller/use-case method parameters, `Pick<...>` payload shapes, domain union types, and `catch (error: unknown)` narrowing. Save deeper generic/infrastructure typing work for a later pass.\n 421 - When moving a raw `fetch(...)` path onto the shared `apiService`, verify the shared service actually supports the response shape you need first (for example `blob` or `text`). Extend the central service/options once, then migrate consumers; otherwise you create avoidable typecheck fallout in every caller.\n 422 - When converting an API client from raw returns to `Result<T, ApiError>`, immediately sweep all helper hooks that call it indirectly (especially shared file-upload logic in writing-tool hooks). These indirect consumers are easy to miss and show up later as repetitive type errors.\n 423 - In tests for query-backed Zustand/store hooks, avoid asserting derived selection state that only becomes true after a refetch updates the query data. Prefer asserting stable boundaries such as returned mutation values, cache invalidation, or explicit setter calls unless the refetch is part of the test setup.\n 424 - When converting an API client or helper from raw values to `Result<T, ApiError>`, sweep prefetch/query consumers as well as mutation callers. Component-level `prefetchQuery` functions and infinite-query loaders are easy to miss and will otherwise cache `Result` objects instead of domain data.\n 425 - When renaming or upgrading a shared helper (`runAuthenticatedClientCall` → `runAuthenticatedResultCall`, etc.), immediately update Jest mocks in existing tests to match the new export shape. Refactors often fail first in tests because the mock contract lags behind the production import.\n 426 - In hook/unit tests for rich domain collections (for example `ProjectContent` unions), prefer lightweight doubles plus explicit `as any` casts at the test boundary over constructing fake full entities. This keeps tests focused on hook behavior instead of unrelated domain initialization requirements.\n 427 - In this repo, broad content searches are often more reliable than highly constrained `path_glob` searches for final cleanup sweeps. If a targeted glob returns surprising "no matches" results, retry with a simpler repo-wide content search and then open the concrete files directly.\n 428 - When converting a direct-`fetch(...)` component/page to `apiService`, a small full-file rewrite is often safer than stacking many local replacements. These components usually get shorter once auth, headers, and parsing are centralized, and whole-file rewrites avoid leaving mixed old/new request logic behind.\n 429 - For Jest component tests in this repo, prefer locally mocking ESM-heavy visual dependencies like `lucide-react` rather than widening global transform settings. It keeps tests focused on behavior and avoids unnecessary parser/config churn.\n 430 - When adding new TypeScript Jest tests, do not assume `@testing-library/jest-dom` matcher types are visible to `tsc` just because the runtime setup file requires them. Either ensure the ambient typings are configured explicitly or prefer plain Jest assertions (`toBeTruthy`, equality, length checks) in new tests to avoid typecheck-only failures.\n 431 - **Database Migration Insights (NoSQL to SQL)**:\n 432     - When migrating from NoSQL (Firestore) to SQLite (Drizzle), use `text` columns for JSON fields (arrays, nested objects) and handle serialization/deserialization explicitly in the repository layer to maintain domain entity compatibility.\n 433     - After changing the database provider, immediately update the DI container (`src/infrastructure/di/container.ts`) and run `npm run typecheck` to catch method signature or argument order mismatches.\n 434     - **Repository Interface Verification**: Before implementing new repositories during a technology switch, always cross-reference the exact method signatures in the Domain Repository Interfaces (`src/modules/*/domain/repositories/*.ts`). SQL and NoSQL repository implementations often differ in argument order or data mapping, and catching these early saves time during the typecheck/fix cycle.\n 435     - **API Client Synchronization**: When updating domain interfaces (e.g., adding dashboard metrics), remember to update the corresponding interface in the `api-client` adapter. Unlike a direct repository change, the frontend often relies on these client-side interface definitions, and missing them causes `tsc` failures in components.\n 436     - **Local Auth Transition**: Replacing Firebase Auth with local SQLite auth is a "full-slice" migration. It requires: 1) Backend storage (password hashes), 2) Sign-in logic (JWT/bcrypt), 3) API routes (`/login`, `/signup`, `/me`), 4) `AuthContext` refactoring to use local `/me` instead of SDK listeners like `onAuthStateChanged`.\n 437     - **Session Flow**: Local auth relies on HTTP-only cookies. After successful login/signup, the API should set the cookie, and the frontend should typically perform a `window.location.href` refresh to ensure `AuthContext` re-runs its initialization flow and picks up the new user state.\n 438     - **Authentication Fallback**: The `withAuth` middleware (`src/lib/utils/api-utils.ts`) supports both `Authorization: Bearer <token>` headers and `__session` cookies. This provides compatibility between standard API clients (token-based) and direct browser-based component calls (cookie-based). If a r\n 439 ... [output truncated]\n````'}
